          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has a raised blood pressure reading but no diagnosis of hypertension, which chapter does that belong in?",
          "answer": "If someone has raised blood pressure readings but no diagnosis of hypertension, this situation is classified as an abnormal finding rather than a disease. According to the ICD-10-CM coding system, these readings fall under **Chapter 18**, which includes symptoms, signs, and abnormal findings (codes R00-R99).\n\nSpecifically, non-diagnosed elevated blood pressure readings are documented under the codes R00 to R09, which encompass cardiovascular symptoms. The relevant code for an elevated blood-pressure reading without a hypertension diagnosis is **R03.0** (elevated blood pressure reading without diagnosis of hypertension). The document \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\" clarifies that such elevated readings are not assigned the I10 code used for diagnosed hypertension, as the classification hinges on documented diagnoses rather than simply the numeric values of blood pressure readings. \n\nIn summary, raised blood pressure readings without a hypertension diagnosis belong in Chapter 18 of the ICD-10-CM.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
